=== layout: subsection

# 5. AAV Therapy

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. What Makes AAV Different?

@body

- Adeno-associated virus (AAV) vectors have a protein capsid and a small single-stranded DNA genome.
- Standard AAV payloads are limited to about 4.7 kb, including the therapeutic sequence and regulatory parts.
- In many non-dividing target cells, vector DNA persists mainly as episomes rather than integrating at a chosen chromosome site.

=== layout: four-panels

# 5. Four AAV Design Questions

@top-left

## Does it fit?

Gene, promoter, and regulatory sequences all compete for capacity.

@top-right

## Where does it go?

Capsid, route, and dose influence tissue exposure.

@bottom-left

## How fast?

Self-complementary designs can speed expression but sacrifice payload room.

@bottom-right

## How long?

Non-dividing cells can retain episomes; cell division dilutes them.

=== layout: two-panels

# 5. Tissue Is Part of the Design

@left

## Retina or CNS

Local delivery can place a small dose near a defined target. Limited access does not remove the need to check spread and immune response.

@right

## Liver or muscle

Systemic exposure can make the target reachable, but it also makes dose, antibodies, and unintended tissue uptake important.

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. AAV: Payload and Immunity

@body

- Neutralizing antibodies can block vector entry before a dose reaches target cells.
- Capsid choice can alter targeting, but no capsid is universally tissue-specific.
- Redosing may be difficult after an immune response.
- AAV is a delivery platform, not a guarantee of durable benefit. Name the molecular measurement and functional measurement you would use to test a proposed therapy.

=== layout: subsection

# 6. Retrovirus and Lentivirus

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Why Integrate a Payload?

@body

- Retroviral vectors make a DNA copy of their RNA payload and integrate it into a host chromosome.
- Gamma-retroviral vectors work most readily when target cells divide. Lentiviral vectors can also deliver a pre-integration complex to many non-dividing cells.
- Integration can support durable expression, but it creates a permanent genomic insertion that must be investigated.

=== layout: two-panels

# 6. Vector Biology, Not Magic

@left

![HIV-vector teaching figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/hiv_vector_legacy.png)

@right

- A lentiviral vector borrows delivery functions from HIV-like viruses while removing genes needed for viral replication.
- The therapeutic payload is not "HIV treatment"; it is a redesigned vector system.
- The relevant contrast is stable integration versus adenoviral gene addition, not "good virus" versus "bad virus."

=== layout: two-panels

# 6. Ex Vivo Adds Control

@left

![lentiviral-delivery figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/lentiviral_delivery_legacy.png)

@right

- Collect hematopoietic stem cells or T cells.
- Modify and characterize them outside the body.
- Measure viability, vector copies, identity, and function before return.
- Long-lived stem cells and T cells can pass an intended effect to descendants.

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Integration and Safety

@body

- An insertion can disrupt a gene or activate nearby growth-control genes: *insertional mutagenesis*.
- Self-inactivating vector designs and cellular promoters reduce some risks; they do not eliminate the need for follow-up.
- A suicide-gene switch is a contingency: if the engineered cells become dangerous, a matching treatment can selectively remove them.
- Why is a controlled ex vivo product often more practical to test than an in vivo integrating vector?

=== layout: subsection

# 7. Liposomes and Lipid Nanoparticles

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. A Nonviral Envelope for Nucleic Acid

@body

- Liposomes are phospholipid bilayer vesicles. Lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) are engineered mixtures, often including ionizable lipids, suited to nucleic-acid delivery.
- These particles can protect RNA or DNA outside cells and help cargo cross the cell membrane.
- Nonviral does not mean no delivery problem: formulation, dose, tissue exposure, and toxicity still matter.

=== layout: two-panels

# 7. Structure Determines Behavior

@left

![liposome-structure figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/liposome_structure_legacy.png)

@right

- Particle size and composition influence circulation and tissue uptake.
- A near-neutral particle at physiological pH can become more interactive in an acidic endosome.
- PEG-containing lipids can affect circulation and immune recognition; the formulation is a system, not one ingredient.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![7. Liposomes and Their Cargo](assets/set5_gene_therapy/liposome_structure_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 7: Liposomes and Their Cargo

=== layout: two-panels

# 7. The Endosomal-Escape Bottleneck

@left

![liposome-entry figure](assets/set5_gene_therapy/liposome_entry_legacy.jpg)

@right

- Uptake is not delivery to the cytosol.
- Cargo trapped in an endosome may be degraded or recycled.
- Ionizable lipids are designed to help a fraction of cargo leave that compartment.
- Measure target-cell protein or RNA response, not merely fluorescent particle uptake.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![7. From Uptake to Delivery](assets/set5_gene_therapy/liposome_entry_legacy.jpg)

@caption

Topic 7: From Uptake to Delivery

