Topic 7: Delivery Through Lipid Carriers

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Compare Delivery Platforms

@body

| Question | Viral vector | Lipid particle |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Repeat dosing | Can be limited by anti-vector immunity | Often possible, but formulation and toxicity matter |
| Payload behavior | Depends on vector | Especially useful for transient RNA cargo |
| Major bottleneck | Targeting, immunity, and vector biology | Endosomal escape and tissue delivery |
| Evidence needed | Expression, biodistribution, immune safety | Same, plus particle consistency and RNA integrity |

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Nonviral carriers and cellular uptake](assets/set5_gene_therapy/nonviral_carriers_legacy.png)

@caption

Topic 7: Carriers and Cell Entry

=== layout: subsection

# 8. RNA Medicines

=== layout: one-panel

# 8. Treating the RNA Message

@body

- RNA medicines can change protein production without permanently changing a chromosome.
- siRNA guides a cellular complex to a matching RNA, lowering that message.
- Antisense oligonucleotides (ASOs) bind a target RNA to block it, recruit degradation, or alter splicing.

=== layout: two-panels

# 8. siRNA and ASO Ask Different Questions

@left

## siRNA

- Is the target RNA reduced?
- Does the matching protein fall?
- Are close off-target RNAs affected?

@right

## ASO

- Is translation blocked, RNA degraded, or a splice choice changed?
- Does the intended exon or isoform change?
- Does the altered protein produce the expected phenotype?

=== layout: four-panels

# 8. Delivery Determines the Use Case

@top-left

## Liver

Chemical targeting groups can help deliver some oligonucleotides to hepatocytes.

@top-right

## CNS

Spinal-fluid dosing can bypass some barriers, while creating a different monitoring problem.

@bottom-left

## Duration

An RNA effect is usually temporary. Follow RNA, protein, and a disease-relevant marker over time.

@bottom-right

## Safety

Check the tissues actually exposed, including liver tests or injection-site effects when relevant.

=== layout: one-panel

# 8. Measure Before Claiming

@body

- A lower target RNA is a mechanism measurement. It does not by itself prove clinical benefit.
- A splice assay can show that an ASO changed RNA processing. A protein or functional assay tests the consequence.
- For either class, delivery, chemical stability, sequence specificity, and repeated-dose tolerance are central design constraints.
@citation

FDA: https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/cellular-gene-therapy-products
